							<table id="pnlMain" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#3366FF" border="2" height="100%" width="100%" background="http://coven.guildwizard.com/pics//////////////BackgroundPart1Test.jpg"><tr><td><font color="Black">
	
								<span id="lblHtml"><font color="Black" size="2"><P><STRONG>Membership</STRONG></P>
<P>Coven of Defiance officers will not actively spam zones with requests to add<BR>members or actively recruit.  Current members in good standing should refer<BR>new members.  Referrals should be based on fit.  Every effort will be made<BR>to ensure there are enough members on line at any given time to have at<BR>least one other compatible member available to form a group.  If you group<BR>with someone you feel exemplifies our philosophy then this is probably<BR>someone we would like to have with us.  People who are willing to help<BR>others unselfishly, who take the initiative to form groups and display<BR>leadership are good additions.  Avoid those individuals who participate in<BR>slandering, bragging, cheating, or insulting others.  We do not want to<BR>recruit people who have selfish goals or appear to have little patience or<BR>will not contribute in some way.  Applications are accepted through<BR>interview for those who actively pursue membership.</P>
<P>Currently, CoD is a primarily midlevel guild.  Events and groups are planned<BR>with current membership in mind, for this reason, persons less than levels<BR>20 should not apply.  However, the final decision is up to the recruiting<BR>officer.  As the guild grows in level, events will be altered to fit the<BR>membership.  Every attempt will be made to have something for everyone,<BR>levels 25+ at least once per month.</P>
<P>You may remove yourself from the guild at any time for any reason and<BR>hopefully without making a scene.  The guild leader or officers may remove<BR>you at any time.  Removable offenses include: sabotaging an event, violating<BR>good conduct and “play nice” rules (such as camp stealing, kill stealing,<BR>purposely training someone, shouting unbecoming messages), slandering other<BR>members, scamming, begging, profanity, or creating a guild disturbance.<BR>There may be occurrences of someone being asked to remove himself or herself<BR>if they develop irreconcilable differences with another member.  If anyone<BR>believes they are removed wrongly, they may appeal to the mediations<BR>officer.  Only the removing officer may reinvite.  Officers are encouraged<BR>to leave notice with the guild leader before removing themselves.</P>
<P><STRONG>Events</STRONG></P>
<P>Guild events will be planned by guild membership or an event can be<BR>collaborated with an allied guild.  Any member may plan an organized event<BR>through the guild calendar.  Guild administrators will approve events to<BR>ensure they do not conflict with other scheduled events.  Officers are<BR>expected to host one event per month.  An event can be essentially anything<BR>planned that involves the guild membership as a group or whole.  A typical<BR>event lasts from 3-4 hours but may go longer if there are adequate resources<BR>willing to stay.</P>
<P>The focus of any event is up to the event coordinator.  Any guild member who<BR>attends an event will follow the event leader’s directions.  Event<BR>coordinators will not be criticized for trying and the only event considered<BR>to be a failure are the ones that don’t happen.  Organizing and leading an<BR>event is complex, usually quite stressful and hectic.  People who arrive at<BR>an event are expected to stay the duration and follow directions.  Attendees<BR>should not expect loot, experience or anything beyond being there to support<BR>the guild and the organizer.  The main focus should always be to have fun<BR>together.  Sometimes organizing groups and buffs can take considerable time.<BR>Do not come to an event unless you are willing to spend some time getting<BR>rolling.  An event may be cancelled if the proper resources are not<BR>available.</P>
<P><STRONG>Basic guidelines for raiding:</STRONG></P>
<OL>
<LI>Print a map and be at the assigned area at the start of the raid.  Do not<BR>ask directions or expect transport after the event has started.  If you<BR>think you may arrive late, camp in the raid start location before you log<BR>off, you will be added if at all possible.</LI>
<LI>Do not send the organizer or group leaders unnecessary tells during an<BR>event whether you are participating or not.</LI>
<LI>Do not move ahead unless you are told to do so.  Never move in beyond the<BR>group.  Keep in mind that sometimes the raid leader will have support<BR>available to help secure a camp, just because one or several persons appear<BR>to be moving ahead do not assume it is ok for you.</LI>
<LI>Do not assume that if there is a lull, it is ok to start pulling mobs on<BR>your own unless you are the designated puller and have the go ahead from the<BR>event leader.</LI>
<LI>Know who the main assist is.  Always use an /assist hotbutton.  If there<BR>are multiple adds, pay attention to traffic control.</LI>
<LI>Do not loot unless you are told or designated to do so.</LI>
<LI>Support the event coordinator and group leaders even if you do not agree<BR>with their method.</LI>
<LI>Do not AFK for long periods unless it is deemed a good time to do so.<BR>(you will be removed from the event until you return and participate)  If<BR>you do need to AFK, put your AFK flag on and let the group leader know you<BR>are not going to be there.</LI>
<LI>If the raiding party is on the move, be ready to move with them.  Try not<BR>to AFK on the way to a camp.</LI>
<LI>Do not gate away from danger unless you plan not to return to an event.<BR>Only evac when told to do so by your group leader.  Sometimes recovery can<BR>be relatively painless if we all stand and die together.</LI>
<LI>Looting rules are up to the organizers and group leaders, usually<BR>involving a Master Looter.  Classes using group component type spells should<BR>be reimbursed for before split is done.  Those who need them should purchase<BR>Coffins themselves unless they can be purchased with raid funds.  Event<BR>leaders should not have to buy coffins unless they are raiding something<BR>that will benefit them specifically (epic or quest mobs).  We will never<BR>leave anyone behind.</LI>
<LI>Do not expect a split if you are not able to stay for the entire event.<BR>Do not volunteer to master loot if you plan to leave an event.  If you do<BR>master loot, it is your responsibility to be fair.  Never loot no-drop items<BR>without consulting your raiding party first.</LI>
<LI>Item drops should go to classes that can use them first if they will be<BR>an equippable upgrade, otherwise they will be lottoed.  Do not roll on<BR>something you do not plan to use.  (equipping alts is fine, selling an item someone needs is discouraged)</LI>
<LI>Do not leave an event early because you don’t like it.  Support your<BR>organizer as if it were your event or don’t come.</LI>
<LI>Be patient, sometimes things don’t go the way they should.  This is what<BR>makes the game great.  Don’t point fingers or criticize, we are all allowed<BR>our moments as human beings.\</LI>
<LI>Check the calendar and motd! Don’t ask “what raid?” while people are<BR>assembling or raiding.  Expecting people to type details to you specifically<BR>is a distraction.</LI>
<LI>Guests and allies attending Coven raids will be treated as guild members<BR>during the event.</LI></OL>
<P> </P>
